1) 
Option A — catchError on the outside (wrong place):

trigger$.pipe(
  switchMap(t => apiCall(t)),
  catchError(err => of(fallbackValue))
).subscribe(...)

Here's the thing people get wrong: in RxJS, pipe() builds one continuous stream. switchMap isn't a separate box that can fail on its own and let trigger$ keep going — its output is the stream. So when the inner apiCall errors:

The error propagates out through switchMap.
switchMap's subscription to trigger$ (the source) gets torn down as a side effect — RxJS treats an error as "this stream is over."
catchError catches that error and substitutes of(fallbackValue) in its place — but it's replacing the entire pipeline, not just that one bad response.
of(fallbackValue) emits once and completes. Nothing is listening to trigger$ anymore.

So visually: you type one more character, click one more button, nothing happens. The stream is dead, silently. You got one fallback value and then permanent silence — no crash, no error in the console, just a UI that stopped responding to input.

2) 
Option B — catchError on the inside (correct place):

trigger$.pipe(
  switchMap(t => apiCall(t).pipe(
    catchError(err => of(fallbackValue))
  ))
).subscribe(...)

Now catchError is wrapped around just the inner apiCall(t), inside the projection function that switchMap calls per trigger. When that one call errors:

The error happens inside the small inner pipe.
catchError catches it right there and swaps in of(fallbackValue) for that inner observable only.
From switchMap's perspective, that inner observable just completed normally (with a fallback value instead of a real one). switchMap's subscription to trigger$ was never touched.
The next trigger comes in and works completely normally.

