## 10. Interview-Ready Answer

"All four are flattening operators — they take a stream of values, map each one to an inner Observable, usually some async call, and flatten the inner emissions back into a single output stream. The only difference between them is how they handle a new source value arriving while a previous inner Observable is still active. `mergeMap` runs everything concurrently — no cancellation, no queuing, all inner Observables run in parallel and emit whenever they resolve. `concatMap` queues them — it waits for the current inner Observable to fully complete before starting the next, so everything's processed strictly in order, one at a time, and nothing is dropped. `switchMap` cancels the previous inner Observable the moment a new source value arrives, so only the latest one's result is ever kept — anything from a superseded, in-flight call is thrown away. `exhaustMap` does the opposite of `switchMap` — it ignores new source values entirely while one's still in flight, keeps the current one running to completion, and only picks up a new value once it's free again.

For the three scenarios: search-as-you-type is `switchMap`, because only the newest search term's results matter — if a slower, earlier request resolves after a newer one, its stale results must not overwrite what's on screen, so cancelling superseded requests is exactly the right behavior. Payment processing is `exhaustMap`, and this one really matters to get right — you never want to cancel a payment request that might already be processing on the server, which rules out `switchMap`, and you also never want to queue up and actually fire a second real charge if the user double-clicks, which rules out `concatMap`. `exhaustMap` just ignores the extra click entirely while the first payment's in flight, which is the safe behavior. File upload is typically `concatMap`, since files usually need to upload one at a time, in a predictable order, without overwhelming the server with many large simultaneous uploads — though if the files are genuinely independent and maximum parallel throughput is the actual goal, `mergeMap` with an optional concurrency limit is the alternative."

## 11. Quick Revision

- All four: map each source value to an inner Observable, flatten its emissions into the output — they differ only in overlap handling.
- `mergeMap`: run all inner Observables concurrently, no cancellation, no order guarantee.
- `concatMap`: queue, run strictly one at a time, in order — nothing dropped, everything eventually processed.
- `switchMap`: cancel the previous inner Observable when a new source value arrives — only ever the latest wins.
- `exhaustMap`: ignore new source values while one's in flight — the current one wins, new ones are dropped.
- Search-as-you-type → `switchMap` (stale results must be discarded).
- Payment/submit → `exhaustMap` (must not cancel an in-flight charge, must not queue a duplicate one).
- Sequential file upload → `concatMap` (ordered, one at a time); independent parallel uploads → `mergeMap` (optionally concurrency-limited).
- An unhandled error in the inner Observable kills the whole outer subscription, for all four operators — always put `catchError` *inside* the projection function (wrapped around the inner Observable), never only on the outer pipe, so one failure doesn't permanently stop every future value.

## 12. Memory Trick

Picture four different receptionists handling a line of visitors, where each visitor is a new source value and each visit takes time behind a closed door. **mergeMap's** receptionist waves every visitor straight through — all doors open at once, full concurrency, whoever finishes first comes back out first. **concatMap's** receptionist makes everyone wait in a strict single-file line — only one door open at a time, the next person doesn't go in until the current one comes out. **switchMap's** receptionist, the instant a new visitor walks in, yanks the current visitor out of the room mid-conversation and sends the new one in instead — only the latest visitor ever actually finishes. **exhaustMap's** receptionist puts up a "BUSY, come back later" sign the moment someone's already inside — visitors who show up during that time are turned away entirely, not even asked to wait.
